## How to Work Here

These apply to every stage: specs, models, and code.

### Discussion first
The default mode is discussion, not implementation. Do not write implementation code
until the relevant gate is met. If the user asks for something whose spec is not
ready, say so and help finish the spec.

### Minimal shape
Before committing anything - a spec, a feature, a statement, code - work out its
minimum usable shape: the least that satisfies the decisions on record and the user's
intent. Write that and no more.

- Nothing inferred goes in silently. Functionality, options, configurability, future
  hooks, and implementation detail the user did not decide are raised as questions.
- Everything should be able to answer "what is this from?" with a decision, a note, or
  something the user said. If it cannot, it does not belong.
- Prefer removing to adding. When a hazard needs a response, first offer the one that
  shrinks the system.
- Minimal governs the design. It never governs the formal layer: do not trim
  invariants, contracts, or sweep output for brevity.

### Raised, not added
Whenever you hand the user a spec or a model, include a short list titled "Raised, not
added": what you were tempted to include and left out. The user accepts, parks, or
ignores each. Only accepted items go in.

### Park
"Park" sets a topic down without losing it, so attention can stay on the work at hand.
When the user says "park <something>" anywhere in a message, even mid-thought in the
middle of other work:

1. Add a row to `docs/spec/parked.md`, creating it from the template if it is missing.
   Write their words exactly as said, then what we were working on at that moment.
   Their words are a reminder to them: do not rephrase, expand, or interpret them.
2. Set Take up yourself. If unsure, `stage one`. Do not ask.
3. Confirm in one line (`Parked P7.`) and carry on with the rest of the message as if
   the park were not there. Do not discuss the parked topic, ask about it, or act on it.

You may park your own items the same way, when something is worth raising but would
interrupt the work. Say so in one line at the end of your reply.

Bring parked items back. Read the list at the start of a session. At natural pauses,
name the ones that are due. Before any exit test or gate, clear the ones due there. An
item closes only when it has been taken up and its result written where it belongs, or
when the user drops it. Record which in its Status. Never close one silently.

### Raise what was not asked
You have a duty to raise facts, hazards, and consequences the user did not ask about.
Raising puts a question in front of the user; it never changes a spec. Before leaving
a topic, say what the user did not ask that matters. Record a "no" like any decision.

### Sweep; do not trust your own list
Your sense that a list is complete is not evidence. Where a list must be complete, use
a sweep: separate fresh agents, each given one step of one lens, then a merge. The procedure,
lenses, and wording are under "Sweeps" in `docs/verification-strategy.md`.

- **Two sweeps are required:** the claims, at the bridge, and every way a feature's
  state can change, when it is formalized.
- **At two moments, ask the user whether to sweep:** trust assumptions, for the project
  and for each driver, and any reopening that adds surface.
- **Anywhere else,** sweep only a list where a gap would be silent, costly, and
  impossible to derive mechanically. If a tool or a search can produce the list, use
  that.
- **Never skip one silently and never launch one silently.** Say what will be swept,
  with which lenses, and how many agents it will start, and get a yes. For a small subject you may
  propose fewer lenses: name them and ask. Never split an opposite pair.
- **A sweep is real or it is not a sweep.** Considering the lenses yourself in one
  reply is not one. Do not call it one, and do not claim completeness from it.
- **Ask the user for input the way "Asking the human" says:** open questions, no menus,
  no examples from the project's own field, no pressing. Never ask for "lenses". Give
  the introduction before the first sweep in a project.

### Mark what you only believe
State where a fact came from, in the sentence: `[source: ...]` or `[believed]`. Never
give a remembered fact in the same voice as a checked one.

